<pdd-reason>Shared grammar for the BREAKING-CHANGE opt-out directives that let a prompt author authorise a specific change the conformance gates would otherwise block.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-interface>
{
  "type": "module",
  "module": {
    "functions": [
      {"name": "_env_flag_enabled", "signature": "(name: str) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_parse_llm_bool", "signature": "(value: str) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_strip_yaml_front_matter", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> str", "returns": "str"},
      {"name": "_iter_breaking_change_directives", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> List[str]", "returns": "List[str]"},
      {"name": "_prompt_breaking_change_removed_symbols", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> Set[str]", "returns": "Set[str]"},
      {"name": "_prompt_breaking_change_signature_symbols", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> Set[str]", "returns": "Set[str]"},
      {"name": "_prompt_allows_breaking_change", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> bool", "returns": "bool"},
      {"name": "_prompt_has_breaking_change_marker", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> bool", "returns": "bool"}
    ]
  }
}
</pdd-interface>

% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the BREAKING-CHANGE directive
% grammar in `pdd/conformance/directives.py`.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Responsibility
  Turning a prompt's text into the two allow-lists the conformance gates consult:
  symbols the author authorised removing, and symbols whose signature the author
  authorised changing. Plus the `PDD_*` environment-flag readers.

% Non-responsibilities
  No gate decisions. The test-churn gate has its own verb-object grammar and owns
  it. Descendant expansion - treating a permitted class removal as permitting its
  members - belongs to the public-surface gate, not here.

% Vocabulary
  - **directive**: a line whose first non-whitespace tokens are
    `BREAKING-CHANGE:`. The **tail** is everything after that marker.
  - **anchored**: matched only at the start of a line, so prose mentioning the
    marker mid-sentence is inert.

% Contract rules
  R1. Only anchored directives count. A line such as
      `Use BREAKING-CHANGE: remove old_helper to opt out` MUST NOT register.
  R2. Directives MUST be read from the prompt BODY only. Leading YAML front
      matter is stripped first, so an indented directive inside metadata cannot
      authorise anything. A leading UTF-8 BOM is stripped whether or not front
      matter follows. An UNTERMINATED opening fence MUST be left alone rather
      than swallowing the body.
  R3. A removal directive's tail MUST begin with `remove`, `delete`, `drop` or
      `rename` - any of the `-s`/`-d`/`-ing` inflections, case-insensitively.
  R4. A signature directive's tail MUST begin with a `change` inflection followed
      by one of `signature`, `signatures`, `api`, `contract`.
  R5. After the verb the remainder MUST be a comma-separated list of dotted
      Python identifiers, each optionally wrapped in backticks, single quotes or
      double quotes. Wrappers MUST match on both sides, so `"old_helper'` is
      rejected. A single trailing `.`, `;` or `:` is tolerated.
  R6. Tokens containing embedded whitespace MUST be rejected, so
      `BREAKING-CHANGE: remove old_helper to opt out` authorises `old_helper`
      alone and never `to`, `opt` or `out`. Authors wanting several symbols omit
      trailing prose entirely.
  R7. A bare `BREAKING-CHANGE:` with no verb and no symbols MUST authorise
      nothing. Opt-outs are always scoped to named symbols; there is no global
      off switch.
  R8. Every entry point MUST tolerate `None` and empty input by returning an
      empty result rather than raising.

% Inputs and outputs
  `_env_flag_enabled(name)` is true only when the variable is present and its
  stripped, lowercased value is one of `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`.
  `_parse_llm_bool(value)` reads an LLM-enable flag and treats empty input as
  enabled; `0`/`false`/`no`/`off` disable it, `1`/`true`/`yes`/`on` enable it, and
  anything else is disabled.
  `_prompt_has_breaking_change_marker` reports whether the stripped body mentions
  the marker at all; `_prompt_allows_breaking_change` is a backward-compatible
  alias for it.

% Dependencies
  Standard library only.
